prompt:
Write a TypeScript module (strict mode) with a typed, immutable HTTP request builder. Export:

- `type Method = 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE'`
- `type RequestSpec = { url: string; method: Method; headers: Record<string, string>; body?: string }`
- `request()` -- returns an empty builder.

A builder has chainable methods, each returning a NEW builder (earlier builders are never modified, so one partially built builder can be branched into several requests):

- `url(url: string)` and `method(m: Method)` -- each may be called at most once, in either order;
- `header(name: string, value: string)` -- any number of times; a later value for the same name replaces the earlier one;
- `body(body: string)` -- only after `method(...)` has been called with a method other than `'GET'`; may be called again to replace the body;
- `build(): RequestSpec` -- returns a fresh object with the url, method, a copy of the headers, and `body` only if `body()` was called (otherwise no `body` key at all).

These rules must be enforced at COMPILE time: calling `build()` before both `url` and `method` have been set, setting `url` or `method` twice, calling `body()` before `method()` or after `method('GET')`, or passing an unknown method are type errors. Answer with a single ```ts code block.
